  "_id": "6a7f5797757ee51246381b0a",
  "lab": "6a7f5796eda1a5feaff0b458",
  "name": "Indirect Coomb's Test (ICT)",
  "code": "ICT",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">​<strong>Test Usage:</strong><br>​Prenatal screen for possible maternal-fetal blood incompatibility. Recommended at 28 weeks gestation for Rh-negative women and those women at increased risk of sensitization due to clinical history and/or prior sensitization, such as the previous history of antibody production, history of red cell transfusion, or placental incompetence.​<br></span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">A positive ICT result indicates that the recipient has antibodies in their blood that may attack the donor's red blood cells or the baby's red blood cells. This may indicate the presence of an incompatible blood transfusion or the risk of hemolytic disease of the newborn.​</span><br></p>",
